+*   **Performance:** Tested on Nvidia H100/H200 GPUs [S1, unofficial]. Performance is significantly slower on older GPUs [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Resolution-independent reconstruction, natural handling of near-field wave optics (Fresnel propagation), and smooth volume representation [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Limitations:** High computational cost, sensitive to VRAM limits, requires careful tuning of `netchunk` and `downsampling` [S1, unofficial].
 *   **When to use:** Near-field holotomography where wave optics effects are significant and high-resolution, smooth reconstructions are desired [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Far-field tomography where standard filtered back-projection or voxel-based methods are sufficient and faster [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-HoloINR was introduced by Johannes Gruen, Sebastian Eberle, Imke Greving, Silja Flenner, Martin Burger, Christian G. Schroer, and Johannes Hagemann in 2026 at the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP) [S1, unofficial]. The method addresses the limitations of traditional tomography in near-field setups by integrating holographic phase retrieval with neural implicit representations [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the principles of differentiable volume rendering and inverse tomography, specifically adapting them for the coupled holography-tomography problem [S1, unofficial].
